  "format": 1,
  "about": "What a note writer may touch (PLATFORM-API-1). A note is a .md file, or a .mmd for the doors that edit diagrams, with no path component starting with '.' as HFS+ hears it (its ignorables dropped), and, for markdown, in no folder the scanner skips: exactly views/ or attachments/, at any depth, matched as the scanner matches them, so Views/ and Attachments/, which it lists, hold notes. The scanner hides what the dot rule refuses, Obsidian-style, so a note it lists is a note the writers take. Nor may a note reach knowledge/ by another name: a first component a folding disk hears as `knowledge` (Unicode case folded, HFS+ ignorables dropped) that is not exactly `knowledge`. The bundle's guards hear those names as the bundle too (M53.28), and a note door refuses them without leaning on the guards: it reaches the bundle by its own name or not at all. Only filesystem-independent cases live here; what a disk folds or a symlink reaches is write.rs's own tests. Replayed through the real doors by note_targets_replay_the_shared_cases in src-tauri/src/vault/write.rs and by src/lib/mockIpc.test.ts. The batch_patch, batch_body and batch_create doors are one op of a batch (M60.6), whose writer judges every op's path as these doors do. The set_fields door is the agent's schema-checked field write (M60.4), setting status on the seeded note. A case whose door edits an existing file is seeded with `seed` first unless it says \"seed\": false. `refused` is a fragment both backends' refusals carry, and a refused case must leave every byte where it was; `lands` is the vault-relative path the door wrote; `why` is for the reader. Dedicated canvas doors accept .canvas with the same containment and hidden-path judgement; only those doors write board JSON. Canvas saves seed source as an empty valid board.",
